WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.760
You have to learn on your own
because you're motivated to learn.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:07.440
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and today I
want to talk about artificial intelligence

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.690
and it's application to language learning.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.350
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:17.520
Click on the bell for notifications
and uh, please follow me on a podcast

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.820
service, especially if you're working
on your English, you can carry the

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.900
audio with you and, and, um, I find that
very effective in language learning.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.515
So, artificial intelligence.

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:32.475
Um, you know, I use artificial,
first of all, I should say that, uh,

00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:34.725
October the eighth was my birthday.

00:00:35.055 --> 00:00:40.305
Uh, my son and his family were over and
I got this new golf shirt, so I'm happy.

00:00:40.305 --> 00:00:41.415
77th birthday.

00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:47.684
Anyway, artificial intelligence, so
I use artificial intelligence, uh, in

00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:51.254
the way I learn languages already, and
I think we're just at the beginning of

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.595
what artificial intelligence can do.

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:59.864
I use it, for example, when I, uh, watch
a television program on MTV Lebanon,

00:01:00.345 --> 00:01:06.365
uh, or some other website in Persian
and then I download the MP3 file and

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:10.354
then I put it on Happy Scribe, which
is an automatic transcription service.

00:01:10.354 --> 00:01:13.655
So that is artificial
intelligence as I understand it.

00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:21.244
I see artificial intelligence as using,
uh, basically computing power to do

00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:27.675
things much more quickly and much more
efficiently than humans could do it.

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:35.115
And thereby giving humans the benefit
of this tremendous ability to select and

00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:41.625
to, uh, parse and to divide and, and all
the different things that, uh, that, uh,

00:01:41.655 --> 00:01:44.384
the computer can do to audio and to text.

00:01:44.895 --> 00:01:49.485
Uh, and thereby make it easier for a
learner to learn from that, uh, material.

00:01:50.085 --> 00:01:55.634
So I put, I get these MP3 files, I
get this trans, you know, transcribed.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.580
And then I'm able, and maybe it's a
type of, uh, artificial intelligence,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:08.250
I'm able to import the audio and
the text very easily into LingQ.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.590
So then I have the ability to
study this material on LingQ.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.520
So that's a basic use of
artificial intelligence.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.720
I think every time I go to Google
Translate to translate something,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.200
the machine is translating it for me.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:29.960
So in both cases, the computer is going at
this large corpus of, uh, you know, audio

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.190
or text material that it's been through,
and is able to draw certain conclusions

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:39.830
very, very quickly, much more quickly than
a human could possibly dream of doing.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.390
So you get an instant translation,
which may or not, may not be as

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:49.050
accurate as what a human could do,
but that accuracy is improving.

00:02:49.185 --> 00:02:54.225
So, but, but where do I see auto, or at
least the artificial intelligence going?

00:02:54.405 --> 00:02:58.635
So I did some Googling, uh, of, you
know, how people are using artificial

00:02:58.635 --> 00:03:00.015
intelligence for language learning.

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:04.875
And of course a lot of it relates
to the sort of teacher perspective.

00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:09.704
So we will use artificial intelligence
to provide feedback to the learner

00:03:09.785 --> 00:03:13.394
because the teacher thinks that a
major role for the teacher is to

00:03:13.399 --> 00:03:15.135
provide feedback to the learner.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.580
Personally, I'm not that
interested in feedback.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:23.190
Or artificial intelligence can be used
to evaluate the learner, grade the

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.590
learner, maybe grade the learner in a
way that's more private than or less

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.810
damaging to the learner's self-esteem.

00:03:31.485 --> 00:03:36.285
Uh, or less time consuming for the
teacher, but still evaluating the learner.

00:03:36.495 --> 00:03:41.295
I'm also not very interested in
being evaluated, so neither of those

00:03:41.295 --> 00:03:43.635
two things really turn my crank.

00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:50.235
Then they talk about, you know, coming up
with textbooks that are sort of catered to

00:03:50.235 --> 00:03:52.545
the learner's weaknesses or difficulties.

00:03:52.725 --> 00:03:56.055
Now we're getting a little closer, but
that's not how, how I would use it.

00:03:56.445 --> 00:04:02.205
What I see artificial intelligence and,
and what I think we're gonna try to

00:04:02.205 --> 00:04:07.275
introduce more and more at LingQ is be
because language earning is, is very

00:04:07.275 --> 00:04:12.285
much an individual thing, a personal
thing you can't, you can't expect people

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:16.515
to constantly correct you and guide you
and give you feedback and evaluate you.

00:04:16.605 --> 00:04:18.645
And that somehow that
way you're gonna learn.

00:04:18.855 --> 00:04:21.615
You have to learn on your own
because you're motivated to learn

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:26.865
and therefore you wanna find
content that's at your level.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.710
That's of interest to you.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:33.330
You know all the different ways
in which you can define content

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.290
that's gonna help you learn.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:38.730
If you identify a voice or type of
voice that you like, maybe you can find

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.640
more material with that kind of voice.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.355
For example, you can find more material
uh, of a certain regional accent,

00:04:46.355 --> 00:04:51.395
perhaps maybe I could, uh, I should be
able to dictate to the using, you know,

00:04:51.455 --> 00:04:55.325
voice commands, which is artificial
intelligence and say find me something

00:04:55.325 --> 00:05:02.485
that's 20 minutes long on the, uh, recent,
uh, you know, demonstrations in Iran.

00:05:02.915 --> 00:05:04.625
Maybe some discussion.

00:05:05.175 --> 00:05:10.545
Maybe I could even say that I want the
percentage of new words to be X, sort of

00:05:10.545 --> 00:05:14.445
thing, might be more difficult because
there will be some competing definitions.

00:05:14.594 --> 00:05:18.705
Maybe if you want it too simple,
it can't deal with more complicated

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.915
topics, but these are things
that would have to be worked out.

00:05:22.265 --> 00:05:29.655
Or I want a book, I want a book, an
audio book and a and um, electronic

00:05:29.655 --> 00:05:31.875
book but I don't want it too long.

00:05:32.805 --> 00:05:37.575
And again, I want it on a
certain subject, maybe Iranian

00:05:37.575 --> 00:05:38.835
history or something like that.

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:42.705
Maybe such a thing doesn't exist, but
the amount of time that I spend looking

00:05:42.705 --> 00:05:47.745
for material, if that could be automated,
so someone could automatically find

00:05:47.745 --> 00:05:49.125
that for me, that would be amazing.

00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:53.304
Now we also have text to speech,
which is a form of artificial

00:05:53.325 --> 00:05:57.405
intelligence, but most text to speech
is still not so pleasant to listen to.

00:05:57.495 --> 00:06:00.825
The voice sounds a bit artificial,
but it's getting better all the time.

00:06:00.945 --> 00:06:06.255
Is it possible that we would get to
where, you know, you'd find a text

00:06:07.005 --> 00:06:11.595
somewhere, something written in Wikipedia
or whatever it might be, or a book

00:06:11.595 --> 00:06:15.345
that doesn't have an audio version.

00:06:15.345 --> 00:06:20.205
It's not an audio book, but the quality
of text to speech would be so good that

00:06:20.205 --> 00:06:22.105
would actually be pleasant to listen to.

00:06:22.675 --> 00:06:28.905
A possible use of, um, artificial
intelligence once you have material, say

00:06:28.905 --> 00:06:32.805
that you've imported into LingQ, another
problem I have with a, with a current

00:06:32.805 --> 00:06:38.535
state of artificial intelligence is time
stamping because when I study content at

00:06:38.535 --> 00:06:41.205
LingQ, I like to study in sentence mode.

00:06:41.205 --> 00:06:46.295
So I have a sense, and I would like to
hear the audio just for that sentence.

00:06:47.265 --> 00:06:49.305
But the timestamping is not accurate.

00:06:49.395 --> 00:06:52.155
You know, I use, uh, Happy Scribe.

00:06:52.155 --> 00:06:53.925
It says here you can timestamp.

00:06:54.015 --> 00:07:00.630
And even within LingQ, we have an ability
to, you know, re-timestamp certain texts.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.750
Apparently it's better in other
languages, but in Arabic and

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.400
versions it doesn't work very well.

00:07:05.549 --> 00:07:11.489
So very accurately timestamped material,
matching the sentences that I'm studying

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:16.750
in LingQ, that raises another issue if I
get something automatically transcribed...

00:07:17.669 --> 00:07:21.690
now again, the accuracy of that
transcription is improving all the time.

00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:26.460
So if I have audio material, I
would like to read it, I go to

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.060
Happy Scribe, it's 90% accurate.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.130
That's a problem when you're
learning the language.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.710
Another problem is there's no punctuation.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:38.220
So therefore, some of the sentences
that I would like to study in

00:07:38.225 --> 00:07:42.510
sentence mode are in fact a paragraph
because there was no punctuation.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.390
So as all of this improves, I could
maybe say, I want the sentences to

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.700
be between eight and 12 words long.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:56.790
And so then, The text and the
corresponding audio would be broken

00:07:56.790 --> 00:08:01.950
into sentences that shouldn't be less
than eight words long and shouldn't

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.480
be longer than 12 words long.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.930
I'm just thinking outside the box
here, I don't know to what extent that

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:11.700
is practical or not, but there's a
real advantage in studying material

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:16.710
in what I call sentence mode, Uh, in
terms of, you know, maybe reviewing

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.190
the new characters that were in that
sentence, the ability to sort of

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.520
scramble the sentence and
put it back together again.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.780
The, uh, matching pairs exercise
you can do with the three or four

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.360
words that were in that sentence
that you're still trying to learn.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.909
So if it were possible to find
content that's of interest to

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:39.480
you, the total lesson shouldn't
exceed, let's say 15 minutes.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:46.320
Because you lose your, I don't know,
you start to want to get, get away

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.560
from this text after about 30 minutes
because it takes so long and, and yet

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.640
divided into sentences with the matching
audio uh, that would be a wonderful

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.010
use of, uh, artificial intelligence.

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:03.960
Maybe I could say I'm looking for
certain, um, vocabulary so I could write

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:10.140
something in English with the vocabulary
that I want have it very accurately

00:09:10.454 --> 00:09:12.285
translated into the target language.

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:21.555
Say, you know, Levantine Arabic, and then
I would have very pleasant audio, you

00:09:21.555 --> 00:09:23.865
know, text to speech in Levantine Arabic.

00:09:23.925 --> 00:09:25.155
Is that ever gonna be possible?

00:09:25.365 --> 00:09:25.995
I don't know.

00:09:26.295 --> 00:09:31.050
Or is it always gonna be preferable
to listen to an authentic, you know,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.030
voice, this raises another issue.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.740
When you look at the, uh, things that
people recommend for, you know, potential

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.700
use for, um, artificial intelligence,
they always have these language bots.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:47.220
So you can talk to a robot and the robot
answers you, but how meaningful is that?

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.330
I've never done that.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:52.050
There's no question that if you have
a, a genuine, spontaneous interaction

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.770
with someone in the language you're
learning that is very high resonance.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.640
You're struggling, you're clinging to
every word, that's very high resonance.

00:10:00.135 --> 00:10:03.765
If you have a lesson with a tutor,
it's, it's still a human being.

00:10:03.765 --> 00:10:05.055
It's, it's, it's better.

00:10:05.055 --> 00:10:07.964
It's a little more authentic, but
it's also a little artificial.

00:10:08.025 --> 00:10:13.064
So not quite as good in my view, as that
random, genuine encounter with someone.

00:10:13.814 --> 00:10:16.020
So what about a chat bot?

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.484
Now what about if I'm talking to
a machine that's answering me, I

00:10:19.484 --> 00:10:20.685
don't know how much I'd like that.

00:10:21.165 --> 00:10:24.255
Would I want that chat bot to correct me?

00:10:24.255 --> 00:10:25.814
I don't even want my tutor to correct me.

00:10:25.814 --> 00:10:29.850
I mean she doesn't, I, I,
it's not that I would react

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.410
negatively, but I'm not interested.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:32.490
I'm having a conversation.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.780
I'm enjoying our conversation.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.340
I don't necessarily wanna be corrected.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:39.240
So if you have a chat bot, a bot
correcting you, how useful is that?

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.470
Again, I don't know.

00:10:40.709 --> 00:10:42.600
I'm just throwing stuff out there.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:49.140
Potential uses of AI, already
it has changed the way we learn.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:50.720
I use text to speech all the time.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.790
I use Google Translate all the time.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:56.360
I use these automatic transcription
services when I'm in YouTube

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:59.870
and I'm looking at a, a video
on whatever the subject might

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.520
be, Uh, something from Lebanon.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.170
It's gonna suggest a whole bunch
of other, uh, related type videos.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.570
So is all of that gonna get better?

00:11:08.575 --> 00:11:12.440
So it's becomes easier and easier to
find content of interest and then move

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:16.515
from there to a related content of
interest, all of which helps you sort

00:11:16.515 --> 00:11:21.285
of zoom in on a particular range of, of
vocabulary, all of which makes the job

00:11:21.285 --> 00:11:25.005
of the teacher easier because the teacher
doesn't have to prepare the material.

00:11:25.065 --> 00:11:28.935
You don't have to buy a $200
textbook, such as I saw when

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:30.585
I visited Stanford University.

00:11:30.735 --> 00:11:33.045
Language textbooks selling for $200.

00:11:33.435 --> 00:11:35.535
You can get access to
all of this material.

00:11:36.165 --> 00:11:40.305
And, uh, and even in terms of, uh, the
grammar, I mean, again, AI can help

00:11:40.305 --> 00:11:42.535
you identify this particular word.

00:11:42.865 --> 00:11:47.444
What is, uh, you know, the
conjugation of that verb?

00:11:47.444 --> 00:11:48.704
And, and, uh, so forth.

00:11:49.035 --> 00:11:55.005
You might be able to ask for material
which focuses in on the subjunctive mood

00:11:55.215 --> 00:11:59.925
or the instrumental case or anything
in different languages, and get a whole

00:11:59.925 --> 00:12:05.535
bunch of examples of that particular
structure, that pattern in the language

00:12:05.565 --> 00:12:07.845
for you to listen to and read again.

00:12:08.325 --> 00:12:12.315
It seems to me the potential, the
opportunities there are endless in

00:12:12.315 --> 00:12:17.625
terms of making not only input more
efficient, making it possible to spend

00:12:17.625 --> 00:12:22.275
less time looking for content, more
time on content that is compelling.

00:12:22.365 --> 00:12:25.365
It can be used, I guess, for
evaluation and correction, but

00:12:25.365 --> 00:12:26.565
it's not how I would use it.

00:12:26.655 --> 00:12:31.365
And it can help you zoom in on certain
grammatical aspects of the language

00:12:31.425 --> 00:12:36.060
that, you know, you're having trouble
with so you end up controlling,

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:40.230
through the material you're using to
learn from, you know, what you do,

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.050
where you focus, uh, and so forth.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.300
So I'd be interested
in people's reactions.

00:12:45.305 --> 00:12:47.010
I, I'm not an expert on AI.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:48.240
I've just done a bit of Googling.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.720
I know what I've been using within
LingQ, and I know what works for

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:52.830
me and what I would like to see.

00:12:53.715 --> 00:12:56.805
So, I mean even like if you're listening
to something and you give a voice command

00:12:56.805 --> 00:13:00.045
and say, repeat that five seconds like
you're listening in the car, it didn't

00:13:00.045 --> 00:13:02.295
quite get that repeat and then repeat.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:05.835
You could set that to be a three second
or a five second repeat, and that

00:13:05.835 --> 00:13:07.785
little audio would repeat, for example.

00:13:07.785 --> 00:13:08.445
There's another thing.

00:13:08.475 --> 00:13:10.215
So I look forward to your ideas.

00:13:10.485 --> 00:13:14.175
Uh, those are just some of mine
on the subject of artificial

00:13:14.175 --> 00:13:16.545
intelligence and language.

00:13:17.685 --> 00:13:20.625
Thank you for listening and,
and of course it is related to

00:13:20.775 --> 00:13:22.705
because I'm very much input-based.

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:26.385
It relates to the issue of learning
through listening and reading, but

00:13:26.475 --> 00:13:31.215
greatly improving the efficiency of
that input based learning, making that

00:13:31.215 --> 00:13:35.325
input more and more compelling with
the use of artificial intelligence.

00:13:35.605 --> 00:13:36.615
Thank you.

00:13:36.705 --> 00:13:37.275
Bye for now.

